00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.430
in your life.

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:54.510
So with that, let's start this AMA.

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:56.340
So the first question,

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.410
there's already a couple of
questions that are coming in.

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:00.250
Actually, this was a
question we had from before,

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:01.240
but it's a good one.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:03.627
Adarsh Persona from YouTube had asked,

00:02:03.627 --> 00:02:05.210
"Hi, Sal, I heard that it is possible

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:07.110
to take an SAT in seventh grade.

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:09.420
Do you recommend this?"

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:12.920
My view is it depends on your motivation.

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:16.130
I think if you are a seventh grader

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:17.730
and you're just curious about it

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:22.330
and you wanna have kind of
a run at taking the SAT,

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:24.493
I think that's fine, that's great.

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:27.480
And there's a lot of good
things that can come of that.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:30.650
If you take it, it'll build
your comfort with the test.

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:32.950
You'll essentially be
entering into high school

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:34.140
with a little bit more information,

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:36.550
a little bit more context,
so that's a good thing.

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:38.980
There's also a lot of
programs that you can get into

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:41.640
if you do reasonably well on the SAT

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:45.190
as a middle schooler,
which are pretty neat.

00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:47.700
There's the Duke Talent Search, I believe.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:49.930
I think Johns Hopkins has a program.

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:51.550
I believe Stanford has a program.

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:52.890
So there's a bunch of programs like that.

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:56.330
So if you're someone who likes to be

00:02:56.330 --> 00:02:58.650
on the precocious side of things,

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:01.310
I don't think there's harm in taking it.

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:02.960
Now, the only reason I wouldn't take it

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:06.190
is if you feel some type of
pressure or stress around it,

00:03:06.190 --> 00:03:07.500
if you're like, "Oh, I've gotta take it

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:10.527
'cause everyone else is taking
it, but it stresses me out,"

00:03:11.927 --> 00:03:13.027
then I wouldn't do it.

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:18.210
But generally speaking, I do
advocate it in middle school.

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:19.460
It's not a bad idea to take it.

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:21.110
It just makes you familiar with something.

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:22.410
You learn a little bit about stuff,

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:24.140
you learn what you know,
what you don't know,

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:25.940
and it's completely okay to take a test

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:27.310
that you haven't learned
some of the material.

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:30.650
The SAT, for example, in
math goes up to Algebra II

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:32.540
and really just the core of Algebra II,

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:35.000
and so most seventh
graders would not have seen

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:38.500
that material yet, but it's
interesting to see it on a test

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:40.140
and then see it later in your classroom.

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:43.653
I would also say that if
you're going to take the SAT,

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:46.000
try to brush up a little bit

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:47.380
or learn a little bit of algebra.

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:49.760
You'd be surprised how far it'll get you.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:50.950
But I don't think it's a bad idea.

00:03:50.950 --> 00:03:51.910
I think it could be interesting

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:55.833
as long as you have a good
mindset going into it.

00:03:56.930 --> 00:04:01.930
So from YouTube, Georgie Grogan asked,

00:04:03.537 --> 00:04:05.090
"Do you think Khan Academy works better

00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:07.067
for younger or older people?"

00:04:09.310 --> 00:04:11.920
It depends, we have stuff on Khan Academy

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:13.020
for almost every age group.

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:14.330
We have Khan Academy Kids

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:17.700
and we have some really
cool efficacy studies there.

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:19.450
If you have a three-year-old,
four-year-old,

00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:20.560
five-year-old, six-year-old,

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:22.750
I highly recommend Khan Academy Kids.

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:26.110
If we're talking about
say learning Algebra I

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:31.110
on Khan Academy, I think
we do see a lot of adults

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:34.480
that when they went
through it the first time

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:35.390
in eighth or ninth grade,

00:04:35.390 --> 00:04:37.160
and they might not have
perceived themselves

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:40.043
as strong math students,
when they do it as adults,

00:04:40.043 --> 00:04:41.600
they have a little bit more maturity,

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:43.740
they're a little bit more driven.

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:45.453
They're internally motivated.

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:50.585
They're often very, very
successful at learning things

00:04:50.585 --> 00:04:54.050
that they didn't learn
the first time in school.

00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:55.960
I think if you're a student,

00:04:55.960 --> 00:05:00.047
it's really the degree to
which you can motivate yourself

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:03.550
to really do the work.

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:04.810
And we see a lot of students

00:05:04.810 --> 00:05:06.630
who are very capable of doing that.

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:08.930
In a lot of ways, that skill, that ability

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:10.466
to kind of drive your own learning

00:05:10.466 --> 00:05:13.650
is maybe even more important
than the math itself.

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:15.920
So I wouldn't say it necessarily
works better or worse

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:17.600
for older or younger people,

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:21.333
but I think everyone needs
different degrees of support.

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:25.280
For folks who are very
motivated and very driven,

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:28.580
regardless of their age and
regardless of their capability,

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:31.200
their starting capability, I
think it can work very well.

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:33.260
And then I think other folks

00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:35.350
need different degrees of supports

00:05:35.350 --> 00:05:37.170
and it might be supports
from their teachers,

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:38.500
supports from peers.

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:39.980
Obviously, all of us at Khan Academy

00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:42.520
are constantly thinking of new mechanics,

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:44.030
new ways of showing the information,

00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:46.743
so to make it as engaging as possible.

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:52.793
It's not obvious that older
or younger is a benefit.

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:56.167
From YouTube, AZ asks,

00:05:56.167 --> 00:05:59.670
"What are you planning
to do after quarantine?"

00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:03.950
After quarantine, so I've
said this a couple of times

00:06:03.950 --> 00:06:06.470
in the livestreams, obviously
what we're going through

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:10.140
as a world is a horrible
and bizarre situation,

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:11.640
but there are certain aspects of it

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:14.690
that I'm actually starting to get used to

00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:17.010
and in certain ways enjoy.

00:06:17.010 --> 00:06:20.563
The fact that my kids are at
home, I'm working from home,

00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:25.323
my wife is working from home
two or three days a week now,

00:06:26.450 --> 00:06:28.106
at first, it was hard

00:06:28.106 --> 00:06:30.020
'cause sometimes I'm recording a video

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:32.000
and all of a sudden my
five-year-old's screaming

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:35.110
20 feet away, it can kind of
mess up your concentration.

00:06:35.110 --> 00:06:37.730
But one, I've kinda gotten
used to that a little bit

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:40.110
and now I've started to enjoy

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:42.410
hearing my five-year-old scream

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:45.813
or when I get a break or get lunch,

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:48.930
I get to see them or spend a
little bit of time with them

00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:51.460
or take a break with them
or take a walk with them.

00:06:51.460 --> 00:06:54.913
I'm realizing that time
goes by very, very fast.

00:06:55.980 --> 00:06:58.120
If you are a kid, your
childhood would go by fast,

00:06:58.120 --> 00:06:59.080
and if you're a parent,

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:01.530
your kid's childhood is
gonna go by very fast.

00:07:01.530 --> 00:07:05.580
And I think to be able to spend
this extra time with family,

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:07.060
even if it's incidental,

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:10.210
we see each other while we're eating lunch

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:11.043
and things like that,

00:07:11.043 --> 00:07:11.990
I think that's going to be something

00:07:11.990 --> 00:07:16.743
that I want to retain as
much as I can post-COVID.

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:21.130
I think something about
COVID has made us connect

00:07:21.130 --> 00:07:22.380
with a lot of friends and family

00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:25.240
that we otherwise, frankly,
wouldn't have connected with,

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:28.450
at least as regularly on
Zoom or whatever else.

00:07:28.450 --> 00:07:31.823
I hope that's something
that I keep post-COVID.

00:07:32.860 --> 00:07:33.960
I've always been the person

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:36.010
who likes spending a lotta time outdoors.

00:07:37.288 --> 00:07:38.450
I live out here in Northern California

00:07:38.450 --> 00:07:40.050
where the weather is quite nice.

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:43.477
Pre-COVID I used to go
to gatherings and say,

00:07:43.477 --> 00:07:44.360
"Why aren't we outside?

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:46.170
And people say, "It's 60
degrees, it's so cold."

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:48.897
And I'm like, "No, but
it's outside, it's nature."

00:07:52.257 --> 00:07:53.700
I actually like the fact in COVID

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:57.060
that we're forced to go
outside, and if we do socialize,

00:07:57.060 --> 00:07:59.563
socially distance in
a park, in a backyard,

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:02.827
the stuff I that wanna get.

00:08:06.910 --> 00:08:11.130
Yeah, so those are my big ones.

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:12.900
Obviously, the things
that I miss right now

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:17.093
are my mom lives in New
Orleans, she's by herself.

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:20.700
I would love her to be
able to come and visit us

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:22.060
or us to visit her,

00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:24.360
but obviously, she's in a high risk group

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:25.430
and it would be hard to travel

00:08:25.430 --> 00:08:27.600
on a plane right now because of that.

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:30.280
I think that's the stuff that I can't wait

00:08:31.549 --> 00:08:32.570
we get through that part,

00:08:32.570 --> 00:08:34.950
'cause I think that it's taking a big tax

00:08:34.950 --> 00:08:38.250
on families who are apart right now.

00:08:38.250 --> 00:08:40.420
And I know I'm very lucky

00:08:40.420 --> 00:08:42.410
considering all else
that's going in the world.

00:08:42.410 --> 00:08:44.950
A lot of people are really having,

00:08:44.950 --> 00:08:48.770
above and beyond the COVID
hardship or the COVID fear

00:08:48.770 --> 00:08:50.310
and the quarantine hardship,

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:52.213
are having economic hardships.

00:08:55.574 --> 00:08:57.720
That's gonna be huge when
we get back to normalcy,

00:08:57.720 --> 00:08:59.070
people are gonna be able to, you know,

00:08:59.070 --> 00:09:01.710
restaurants will reopen,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:09:01.710 --> 00:09:04.527
So from Facebook, Tracy Harris is saying,

00:09:04.527 --> 00:09:05.840
"I have some students
who are still struggling

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:07.560
with mastering their
multiplication and division facts.

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:09.450
How can I help them?"

00:09:09.450 --> 00:09:10.550
Great question, Tracy.

00:09:10.550 --> 00:09:13.110
And I would say we do not talk enough

00:09:13.110 --> 00:09:15.990
about the importance of
things like math facts.

00:09:15.990 --> 00:09:17.980
And I think sometimes,

00:09:17.980 --> 00:09:20.890
let's call it the education establishment,

00:09:20.890 --> 00:09:22.280
we don't talk about it as much

00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:24.790
because it feels so simple

00:09:24.790 --> 00:09:26.810
or it's not as sophisticated

00:09:26.810 --> 00:09:28.910
as talking about the deeper standards

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:31.300
of integrating knowledge

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:33.520
and going higher up Bloom's taxonomy.

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:35.890
But I can't tell you how
many young people I've seen,

00:09:35.890 --> 00:09:37.890
I've visited sixth-grade classrooms

00:09:37.890 --> 00:09:41.500
where it's pretty clear that
a majority of the students

00:09:41.500 --> 00:09:44.540
really didn't have fluency in
some of their core math facts

00:09:44.540 --> 00:09:46.150
around addition or multiplication.

00:09:46.150 --> 00:09:48.703
And because of that, it
was just holding them back,

00:09:49.836 --> 00:09:52.750
and even if they knew how to
kind of think about the problem

00:09:52.750 --> 00:09:57.440
and construct equations,
when they hit 9 times 6,

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:00.960
they would just write 56 instead of 54

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:02.430
and get the whole thing wrong.

00:10:02.430 --> 00:10:04.723
And so it's super important.

00:10:05.580 --> 00:10:09.240
I think, to some degree, that
COVID is a really good time

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:10.073
for everyone to make sure

00:10:10.073 --> 00:10:11.820
that they have really strong foundations,

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:13.620
including some middle school.

00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:16.860
I've met high school students
who really don't have fluency

00:10:16.860 --> 00:10:18.493
in their multiplication facts.

00:10:20.890 --> 00:10:24.513
What I would highly recommend
doing is making it social.

00:10:25.530 --> 00:10:27.000
It's kind of very old-school.

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:28.360
Sit down with the flashcards.

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:30.860
I think you can order them on online,

00:10:30.860 --> 00:10:32.480
get some multiplication flashcards,

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:36.257
and sit down with your child
and go through them and say,

00:10:36.257 --> 00:10:38.040
"We're gonna do this 10 minutes a day."

00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:40.640
You know what I've been
doing with my nine-year-old,

00:10:42.220 --> 00:10:44.070
she essentially knows her multiplication,

00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:48.000
but it's not fluent yet, in
the morning at breakfast,

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:49.670
I'll go, "6 times 8 real fast."

00:10:49.670 --> 00:10:52.050
And I'll just do two
or three every morning

00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:54.580
just to see if she can get her fluency.

00:10:54.580 --> 00:10:55.550
She gets a little annoyed.

00:10:55.550 --> 00:10:58.327
She rolls her eyes, but
then she'll go, "48,"

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:01.763
and the 48 is coming out a little faster.

00:11:02.680 --> 00:11:04.290
Anyone who's spent a lot
of time on Khan Academy

00:11:04.290 --> 00:11:08.180
would know I'm not an advocate
generally of memorization

00:11:08.180 --> 00:11:09.970
or rote memorization of things.

00:11:09.970 --> 00:11:11.600
It's very important to have an intuition

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:13.420
and to really understand what's going on,

00:11:13.420 --> 00:11:14.920
but multiplication facts,

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:16.090
obviously the students should understand

00:11:16.090 --> 00:11:19.690
what multiplication is and
how you can even derive

00:11:19.690 --> 00:11:21.650
if you know that 6 times 8 is 48,

00:11:21.650 --> 00:11:22.770
if you wanna know 6 times 9,

00:11:22.770 --> 00:11:24.950
well, you just add
another 6, you get to 54.

00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:26.000
But at some point in your life,

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:28.250
it has to come very, very automatic.

00:11:28.250 --> 00:11:30.320
Otherwise, you're gonna be
spending too much cognitive load

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:33.470
on the multiplication versus
the higher order things

00:11:33.470 --> 00:11:36.430
that you're trying to do.

00:11:36.430 --> 00:11:40.687
So let's see, there's YouTube,
AZ, another question from AZ,

00:11:40.687 --> 00:11:42.360
who asks a lotta questions
about quarantine,

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:43.467
but these are good questions.

00:11:43.467 --> 00:11:46.367
"What's one thing you've
learnt during quarantine?"

00:11:47.530 --> 00:11:48.700
Well, it depends on what you mean learn.

00:11:48.700 --> 00:11:50.350
If it's kind of like learning about life,

00:11:50.350 --> 00:11:51.470
I talked a lot about it,

00:11:51.470 --> 00:11:54.460
is that it's nice to spend
more time with the family,

00:11:54.460 --> 00:11:58.320
nice to connect with
past friends and family

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:00.163
over videoconference.

00:12:01.460 --> 00:12:05.480
I'm finding that, obviously,
the workplace has had to go,

00:12:05.480 --> 00:12:09.560
everyone's workplace where
possible has had to go remote,

00:12:09.560 --> 00:12:12.040
especially people who've
had traditionally desk jobs,

00:12:12.040 --> 00:12:12.873
so to speak.

00:12:14.740 --> 00:12:17.840
It's seeming for certain folks,
it's becoming a lot harder.

00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:19.460
And for other folks they're
actually enjoying it

00:12:19.460 --> 00:12:20.810
because it's less of a commute,

00:12:20.810 --> 00:12:23.340
they're actually feeling more productive.

00:12:23.340 --> 00:12:28.220
So it's been interesting that
a lot of our societal norms,

00:12:28.220 --> 00:12:30.510
which probably wouldn't have
been questioned deeply before,

00:12:30.510 --> 00:12:33.170
but quarantine has
forced us to question it,

00:12:33.170 --> 00:12:37.340
I think I've learned that the
standard assumptions about it

00:12:38.572 --> 00:12:41.260
are maybe not a hundred percent true.

00:12:41.260 --> 00:12:43.340
If you're talking about knowledge

00:12:43.340 --> 00:12:47.080
that I've gained during quarantine,

00:12:47.080 --> 00:12:50.443
let's see, what am I, what am I,

00:12:51.530 --> 00:12:55.190
off the cuff, I don't
have any new factoids.

00:12:55.190 --> 00:12:58.113
I've learned some things in
this livestream situation.

00:13:03.520 --> 00:13:05.920
I'll keep thinking about that question.

00:13:05.920 --> 00:13:10.920
So from Facebook, Misty Schwaller asks,

00:13:11.567 --> 00:13:13.320
"What are you currently reading?"

00:13:13.320 --> 00:13:14.490
So I think I've answered this before,

00:13:14.490 --> 00:13:16.760
and it will tell you
how long this book is,

00:13:16.760 --> 00:13:18.270
the fact that it's been several weeks

00:13:18.270 --> 00:13:19.470
that I'm going to give
you the same answer.

00:13:19.470 --> 00:13:21.400
I'm reading "Shantaram"

00:13:21.400 --> 00:13:25.870
by I think his name is Gregory
David Roberts is the author.

00:13:25.870 --> 00:13:26.980
Now for young people,

00:13:26.980 --> 00:13:29.030
I definitely do not recommend reading it.

00:13:30.310 --> 00:13:33.200
It's about someone who escaped
from prison in Australia

00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:37.490
and then kind of joins the Bombay mafia,

00:13:37.490 --> 00:13:39.130
and in some ways it kind of celebrates

00:13:39.130 --> 00:13:42.150
the vibrancy of life in India in the '80s.

00:13:43.390 --> 00:13:44.820
But there's a lotta darkness to it,

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:45.740
a lotta violence to it.

00:13:45.740 --> 00:13:47.180
I'm normally not a fan of violence,

00:13:47.180 --> 00:13:49.060
but a friend recommended that I read it.

00:13:49.060 --> 00:13:52.120
There's some interesting
philosophical aspects of the book,

00:13:52.120 --> 00:13:53.453
but it's a long book,

00:13:54.440 --> 00:13:56.720
and it gets a little darker
than I normally like,

00:13:56.720 --> 00:14:00.430
but that is what I have been reading.

00:14:00.430 --> 00:14:02.670
I'm almost done.

00:14:02.670 --> 00:14:05.470
I'm eager to think about
what I will read next though.

00:14:05.470 --> 00:14:07.363
I'm open to any recommendations.

00:14:09.370 --> 00:14:11.187
From Facebook, Hussein Rumi,

00:14:11.187 --> 00:14:13.910
"Can you recommend some
books from your shelf?"

00:14:13.910 --> 00:14:16.330
Yes, and some people have
wondered, is that a real shelf,

00:14:16.330 --> 00:14:18.200
with this world of zoom backgrounds,

00:14:18.200 --> 00:14:21.270
but this is very much a real shelf,

00:14:21.270 --> 00:14:26.100
and this shelf has some of
my favorite books on it.

00:14:26.100 --> 00:14:28.400
There's a bunch of science
fiction books here.

00:14:30.970 --> 00:14:33.550
We have "Ender's Game," which is,

00:14:33.550 --> 00:14:35.840
if anyone hasn't read
"Ender's Game," I envy you,

00:14:35.840 --> 00:14:37.910
because you get to read it now.

00:14:37.910 --> 00:14:39.780
And I think this is appropriate

00:14:39.780 --> 00:14:42.260
for anyone maybe in
middle school and above.

00:14:42.260 --> 00:14:44.110
But even if you're 50

00:14:44.110 --> 00:14:46.180
and if you haven't read "Ender's Game,"

00:14:46.180 --> 00:14:47.550
you will love "Ender's Game."

00:14:47.550 --> 00:14:49.560
And the book as normally is the case

00:14:49.560 --> 00:14:50.393
is much better than the movie.

00:14:50.393 --> 00:14:53.860
And I have the other books in the Ender's.

00:14:53.860 --> 00:14:56.260
Actually, I have two copies
of "Ender's Game" here.

00:14:56.260 --> 00:14:59.300
I'd let you borrow one if we
weren't so socially distanced,

00:14:59.300 --> 00:15:03.970
but then I have the
other books in the series

00:15:03.970 --> 00:15:08.910
where Ender travels through
space and comes in contact.

00:15:08.910 --> 00:15:11.200
But I just have a ton, I have
the Foundation series here.

00:15:11.200 --> 00:15:12.900
So that's my science fiction.

00:15:12.900 --> 00:15:14.590
There's a ton that you're not seeing.

00:15:14.590 --> 00:15:17.330
I have, what do I have?

00:15:17.330 --> 00:15:19.270
This "Name of the Wind," Patrick Rothfuss,

00:15:19.270 --> 00:15:22.807
that's more fantasy, but
highly, highly recommend it.

00:15:22.807 --> 00:15:25.410
"Lifespan," David Sinclair,
we had him on as a guest

00:15:25.410 --> 00:15:27.190
a couple of weeks ago.

00:15:27.190 --> 00:15:31.990
Then we have, you probably
can't see all of books here,

00:15:33.137 --> 00:15:35.343
"The Gene" by Siddhartha Mukherjee.

00:15:36.440 --> 00:15:38.000
Yeah, but that's just some of it.

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:40.067
I don't want to bore you all.

00:15:40.067 --> 00:15:43.483
"Forever War," Joe Haldeman,
all interesting books.

00:15:44.350 --> 00:15:48.130
Let's see, from YouTube, SpiritLifter,

00:15:48.130 --> 00:15:49.807
I like your username.

00:15:49.807 --> 00:15:52.730
"What is the best way to
do independent work at home

00:15:52.730 --> 00:15:55.317
if your parents aren't home?"

00:16:00.400 --> 00:16:03.680
Sometimes your parents could
be a distraction. (chuckling)

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:06.530
But sometimes, obviously,
parents can help you stay focused

00:16:06.530 --> 00:16:08.600
and provide the environment.

00:16:08.600 --> 00:16:10.806
So, SpiritLifter, I don't
know how old you are

00:16:10.806 --> 00:16:12.860
or what type of independent work

00:16:12.860 --> 00:16:14.840
you are trying to accomplish at home,

00:16:14.840 --> 00:16:17.733
but my advice is, is try to build a habit.

00:16:18.820 --> 00:16:20.120
I've talked to this researcher

00:16:20.120 --> 00:16:21.610
who talks about the Pomodoro Technique,

00:16:21.610 --> 00:16:23.390
and it's named after this timer

00:16:23.390 --> 00:16:25.760
that's shaped like a
tomato, a Pomodoro tomato.

00:16:25.760 --> 00:16:28.460
And it's this notion of set a regular time

00:16:28.460 --> 00:16:31.060
where you're gonna be able
to focus on your goals,

00:16:31.060 --> 00:16:32.560
try to do it in 20 minute spurts.

00:16:32.560 --> 00:16:34.690
Say, okay, the next 20
minutes, I'm gonna work,

00:16:34.690 --> 00:16:36.210
and then after the 20 minutes,

00:16:36.210 --> 00:16:38.770
you set a timer or put it
on your phone or something,

00:16:38.770 --> 00:16:41.460
on the computer, and then
give yourself a break.

00:16:41.460 --> 00:16:42.930
Let yourself do something you enjoy,

00:16:42.930 --> 00:16:44.200
and then do another 20 minutes.

00:16:44.200 --> 00:16:47.510
And that's a really good
way of not procrastinating.

00:16:47.510 --> 00:16:49.170
And then when you are
doing those 20 minutes,

00:16:49.170 --> 00:16:50.800
and hopefully it's at the same time,

00:16:50.800 --> 00:16:52.850
you have good lighting,
you have good posture,

00:16:52.850 --> 00:16:54.960
you're not slouched on
the bed or on the couch.

00:16:54.960 --> 00:16:57.160
You're doing it at a desk or at a table

00:16:57.160 --> 00:17:01.513
that you just try.

00:17:03.690 --> 00:17:07.210
Well, I would say just the
habit of staying engaged.

00:17:07.210 --> 00:17:09.600
Even if at first, it might
be a little bit difficult,

00:17:09.600 --> 00:17:11.830
slowly that habit will build,

00:17:11.830 --> 00:17:15.350
and you will be able to
make progress on your work.

00:17:15.350 --> 00:17:17.150
I think it doesn't hurt to set goals

00:17:17.150 --> 00:17:20.163
per day, per week, per month.

00:17:22.040 --> 00:17:24.240
Having a checklist for
the day is a great way

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:25.880
to have a bit of a dopamine hit

00:17:25.880 --> 00:17:27.290
every time you accomplish something

00:17:27.290 --> 00:17:28.700
and it kind of builds momentum.

00:17:28.700 --> 00:17:31.320
I've talked about this
in previous livestreams.

00:17:31.320 --> 00:17:33.230
I like to start my day
with some easy wins.

00:17:33.230 --> 00:17:34.950
I always make my bed, 'cause I'm like,

00:17:34.950 --> 00:17:37.970
there, I've already done
something, I've made my bed,

00:17:37.970 --> 00:17:40.970
and it's something I'm gonna
benefit from in about 16 hours.

00:17:42.150 --> 00:17:46.320
I'm pretty ritualistic
about my morning run,

00:17:46.320 --> 00:17:48.230
doing some meditation, doing some pushups,

00:17:48.230 --> 00:17:52.423
brushing my teeth, making the
bed, et cetera, et cetera.

00:17:53.510 --> 00:17:55.280
The more that you can build these habits

00:17:55.280 --> 00:17:57.370
and these patterns and get some wins

00:17:57.370 --> 00:17:59.000
and check some stuff off of your checklist

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:00.760
early on, the better.

00:18:00.760 --> 00:18:04.473
So let's see, other questions.

00:18:05.860 --> 00:18:09.377
So Musaki Yamamoto asked,

00:18:09.377 --> 00:18:11.100
"Hey, Sal, would you
send your kids to school

00:18:11.100 --> 00:18:14.220
if you lived in a high-case state?"

00:18:14.220 --> 00:18:15.970
Interesting question.

00:18:15.970 --> 00:18:19.580
My kids' school is actually
we live I think in a...

00:18:20.680 --> 00:18:22.570
California is having high cases.

00:18:22.570 --> 00:18:25.590
When you look at it per
capita, it's reasonably high.

00:18:25.590 --> 00:18:30.290
Our county is not like
off the charts high,

00:18:30.290 --> 00:18:32.150
but it's having cases.

00:18:32.150 --> 00:18:34.060
The city I live in, in Mountain View,

00:18:34.060 --> 00:18:36.270
actually has relatively low incidents.

00:18:36.270 --> 00:18:38.840
I think they've only had
a hundred or so cases

00:18:38.840 --> 00:18:41.870
in a population of I believe 75,000,

00:18:41.870 --> 00:18:42.800
if I have my numbers right.

00:18:42.800 --> 00:18:43.663
I might be wrong.

00:18:44.800 --> 00:18:46.773
So the way I think about it is,

00:18:48.280 --> 00:18:51.990
the evidence we're seeing is
that the youngest kids are...

00:18:52.870 --> 00:18:54.460
There's not a lot of
evidence that we're seeing

00:18:54.460 --> 00:18:56.660
that they are the spreaders.

00:18:56.660 --> 00:18:57.790
They usually are the spreaders

00:18:57.790 --> 00:18:59.730
for things like the cold and the flu,

00:18:59.730 --> 00:19:01.700
but most of the data that I've seen

00:19:01.700 --> 00:19:04.690
is that they aren't
the spreaders of COVID.

00:19:04.690 --> 00:19:06.010
And those are also the kids

00:19:06.010 --> 00:19:07.840
that probably benefit the most

00:19:07.840 --> 00:19:10.283
from in-person social interaction.

00:19:11.840 --> 00:19:13.890
I'm lucky, as I mentioned, I
live in Northern California.

00:19:13.890 --> 00:19:15.123
The weather's very good.

00:19:16.670 --> 00:19:19.567
Most days in the summer,
the weather's in the 70s

00:19:19.567 --> 00:19:22.790
and in the low 80s, and in
the winter, it's in the 60s.

00:19:22.790 --> 00:19:25.910
And so I think for the younger kids,

00:19:25.910 --> 00:19:27.860
some type of outdoor schooling

00:19:27.860 --> 00:19:29.620
that still takes all the precautions

00:19:29.620 --> 00:19:30.630
could be very interesting.

00:19:30.630 --> 00:19:32.320
And I know the school my kids go to,

00:19:32.320 --> 00:19:34.870
they are looking at that
for the youngest kids,

00:19:34.870 --> 00:19:37.330
for the kindergartners,
first, and second graders.

00:19:37.330 --> 00:19:39.240
And they're trying to get
a variance from the state

00:19:39.240 --> 00:19:41.040
that would allow them to serve that.

00:19:42.900 --> 00:19:43.880
This is just my family,

00:19:43.880 --> 00:19:46.540
everyone has to make the right choices,

00:19:46.540 --> 00:19:48.320
I would be open to that as a parent.

00:19:48.320 --> 00:19:50.120
But I think as you get to older students,

00:19:50.120 --> 00:19:51.820
given where we are at COVID right now,

00:19:51.820 --> 00:19:56.010
especially if you're not
outdoors, if you're indoors,

00:19:56.010 --> 00:19:57.470
I'd be very concerned about that.

00:19:57.470 --> 00:20:00.460
And I wouldn't feel
comfortable sending my kids

00:20:00.460 --> 00:20:02.960
to something that's spending
a lot of time indoors

00:20:04.793 --> 00:20:06.390
given where COVID is.

00:20:06.390 --> 00:20:11.390
But the more time outdoors,
socially-distanced, masks on,

00:20:12.380 --> 00:20:16.330
feels like it could work
in a lotta circumstances.

00:20:16.330 --> 00:20:17.930
But yeah, it's current thinking.

00:20:19.540 --> 00:20:22.290
We're all struggling with
the same questions right now.

00:20:23.500 --> 00:20:25.690
But one thing that I think is interesting

00:20:25.690 --> 00:20:29.000
is even with social
distancing, as I mentioned,

00:20:29.000 --> 00:20:29.833
especially those young kids,

00:20:29.833 --> 00:20:32.910
frankly, everyone needs
social interactions,

00:20:32.910 --> 00:20:36.173
if you have local parks,
if you have a backyard,

00:20:37.350 --> 00:20:40.260
meeting with a small group of folks,

00:20:40.260 --> 00:20:44.800
just one other family, socially
distanced with masks on,

00:20:44.800 --> 00:20:48.200
I think is a good way to get
some of that social interaction

00:20:48.200 --> 00:20:49.900
that I think, frankly, we all need,

00:20:49.900 --> 00:20:51.750
but kids probably need it especially.

00:20:52.670 --> 00:20:55.853
So from Facebook, Julius,

00:20:56.870 --> 00:20:59.667
apologies if I'm not
pronouncing this correctly,

00:20:59.667 --> 00:21:02.920
Surenokis, "Are college-level
math courses in the plans?"

00:21:02.920 --> 00:21:06.250
Well, Julius, we do have some
college-level math courses,

00:21:06.250 --> 00:21:07.800
depending on what you
consider college-level.

00:21:07.800 --> 00:21:11.020
We obviously have statistics, calculus,

00:21:11.020 --> 00:21:12.750
we actually have multivariable calculus.

00:21:12.750 --> 00:21:15.060
We're actually gonna be launching
exercises for that soon.

00:21:15.060 --> 00:21:17.500
And we also have videos
in differential equations,

00:21:17.500 --> 00:21:18.680
linear algebra,

00:21:18.680 --> 00:21:21.293
so it kinda gets into your
freshmen/sophomore level,

00:21:22.800 --> 00:21:24.270
college-level classes.

00:21:24.270 --> 00:21:25.570
I do hope eventually,

00:21:25.570 --> 00:21:27.580
our focus is really
doing the K through 12,

00:21:27.580 --> 00:21:29.370
and then a little bit
of that early college,

00:21:29.370 --> 00:21:31.160
I do hope eventually Khan Academy

00:21:31.160 --> 00:21:33.883
is able to add some of the
more advanced math classes.

00:21:34.820 --> 00:21:37.680
In my mind, that's when math
really gets interesting,

00:21:37.680 --> 00:21:39.350
and I missed some of those courses,

00:21:39.350 --> 00:21:43.040
and I would love nothing
more than to re-dive deep

00:21:43.040 --> 00:21:46.833
into those courses and cover
some of that with you all.

00:21:47.820 --> 00:21:52.820
So from Facebook, Shannon
Seabeck asks or says,

00:21:53.047 --> 00:21:55.180
"Google has started
offering free certificates

00:21:55.180 --> 00:21:56.830
for career paths in
things like data analytics

00:21:56.830 --> 00:21:58.130
or project management.

00:21:58.130 --> 00:21:59.720
Do you think this will
eventually be commonplace

00:21:59.720 --> 00:22:03.060
and a real alternative to a
costly college education?"

00:22:03.060 --> 00:22:05.750
Simple answer is yes.

00:22:05.750 --> 00:22:07.350
I don't think it's gonna be an either/or,

00:22:07.350 --> 00:22:10.100
I think people are gonna try
to do combinations of both,

00:22:10.100 --> 00:22:11.320
but I think you're gonna
see a lot of people

00:22:11.320 --> 00:22:14.010
with college degrees try to
get some of these certificates.

00:22:14.010 --> 00:22:17.270
And if employers like
Google or the Microsofts

00:22:17.270 --> 00:22:18.857
or the Facebooks of the world said,

00:22:18.857 --> 00:22:20.040
"Yeah, we'll hire you

00:22:20.040 --> 00:22:22.520
if you are able to
achieve this certificate,"

00:22:22.520 --> 00:22:24.060
then, yeah, that will
have a lot of cachet,

00:22:24.060 --> 00:22:26.810
maybe even more than a college degree.

00:22:26.810 --> 00:22:29.610
And I think that'll be
healthy if there's more paths,

00:22:29.610 --> 00:22:34.160
more variety of ways to
essentially build your skills

00:22:34.160 --> 00:22:35.420
and have economic opportunity.

00:22:35.420 --> 00:22:36.730
College will always be a path

00:22:36.730 --> 00:22:38.810
and a lot of great things about college,

00:22:38.810 --> 00:22:42.090
but for certain people,
the costs, the time,

00:22:42.090 --> 00:22:44.690
the things that it focuses on
might not be exactly right.

00:22:44.690 --> 00:22:48.050
So I'm always a big fan a larger diversity

00:22:48.050 --> 00:22:50.563
of opportunity and paths.

00:22:51.807 --> 00:22:56.807
So from YouTube, AiyanaAisheForever says,

00:22:57.957 --> 00:22:59.810
"Mr. Sal, I'm really
suffering with the SAT,

00:22:59.810 --> 00:23:00.643
the English section.

00:23:00.643 --> 00:23:02.770
Do you have any tips for it?"

00:23:02.770 --> 00:23:04.360
Well, it depends how much
time you have for it.

00:23:04.360 --> 00:23:05.810
I mean, the English section of the SAT,

00:23:05.810 --> 00:23:08.200
it really is if you read a lot

00:23:11.159 --> 00:23:13.690
and your language usage is solid

00:23:13.690 --> 00:23:16.360
in terms of kind of
grammar language usage,

00:23:16.360 --> 00:23:17.710
you should do okay.

00:23:17.710 --> 00:23:19.210
I think one thing that hurts

00:23:19.210 --> 00:23:20.800
a lot of people is maybe reading speed,

00:23:20.800 --> 00:23:24.790
and there, I would say, just
practice, practice, practice.

00:23:24.790 --> 00:23:27.090
Practice will only benefit you.

00:23:27.090 --> 00:23:29.580
We see that in the data, on
the official SAT practice

00:23:29.580 --> 00:23:32.350
that we do in partnership
with the College Board,

00:23:32.350 --> 00:23:33.740
that the more people practice,

00:23:33.740 --> 00:23:35.480
the more that they do
the recommended activity,

00:23:35.480 --> 00:23:37.230
the more that they take practice tests,

00:23:37.230 --> 00:23:39.730
the more that they really
focus on practicing skills

00:23:39.730 --> 00:23:41.780
at kinda their learning edge,

00:23:41.780 --> 00:23:44.050
those students are growing the most.

00:23:44.050 --> 00:23:45.750
So that's what I would recommend.

00:23:45.750 --> 00:23:48.460
To earlier questions
about how do you do that?

00:23:48.460 --> 00:23:49.650
Build a habit.

00:23:49.650 --> 00:23:51.480
Say every day I'm gonna do,

00:23:51.480 --> 00:23:53.190
if the verbal or the English section

00:23:53.190 --> 00:23:54.400
is what's giving you trouble,

00:23:54.400 --> 00:23:56.600
every day I'm gonna do an hour.

00:23:56.600 --> 00:24:00.150
By hook or by crook, everyday
I'm gonna do an hour.

00:24:00.150 --> 00:24:02.670
And I think after several weeks of that,

00:24:02.670 --> 00:24:04.840
you're definitely gonna
notice improvement.

00:24:04.840 --> 00:24:06.610
Your reading speed is going to improve.

00:24:06.610 --> 00:24:08.830
You're going to just feel more comfortable

00:24:08.830 --> 00:24:10.730
and less stressed in the test as well.

00:24:11.670 --> 00:24:16.670
So there's from Facebook,
Sandra Sand says does Khan...

00:24:18.517 --> 00:24:19.860
Oh, I only have five minutes left.

00:24:19.860 --> 00:24:20.807
Time goes by fast.

00:24:20.807 --> 00:24:23.660
"Does Khan Academy have
a larger strategy or plan

00:24:23.660 --> 00:24:26.347
to support remote learning
during the pandemic?"

00:24:27.240 --> 00:24:30.230
The simple answer, Sandra,
is yes, we are trying.

00:24:30.230 --> 00:24:31.840
We're doing the work
we've always been doing

00:24:31.840 --> 00:24:34.230
with teachers and districts
and parents and families.

00:24:34.230 --> 00:24:35.970
We've been also been doing
these learning plans.

00:24:35.970 --> 00:24:37.930
We've been running webinars
for parents and teachers

00:24:37.930 --> 00:24:40.023
on how to do distance learning.

00:24:42.140 --> 00:24:45.150
Expect an op-ed from me
in a major newspaper soon

00:24:45.150 --> 00:24:49.780
about what good distance
learning could look like.

00:24:49.780 --> 00:24:53.910
We are actually publishing a
study on distance learning,

00:24:53.910 --> 00:24:54.910
not just using Khan Academy,

00:24:54.910 --> 00:24:57.300
but also using live videoconferences.

00:24:57.300 --> 00:24:59.810
What is the best practice there?

00:24:59.810 --> 00:25:01.330
We've obviously just published

00:25:01.330 --> 00:25:03.530
our get ready for grade level courses

00:25:03.530 --> 00:25:06.030
to allow students and teachers

00:25:06.030 --> 00:25:08.210
to make sure students have
all the prerequisites filled,

00:25:08.210 --> 00:25:09.320
so they're ready for grade level,

00:25:09.320 --> 00:25:12.000
any gaps that they might
have accrued over COVID

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:13.360
or even before that.

00:25:13.360 --> 00:25:16.100
I've talked about the
project Schoolhouse.world.

00:25:16.100 --> 00:25:18.650
This is a project, it's not
officially part of Khan Academy.

00:25:18.650 --> 00:25:21.210
It's a separate volunteer-driven
project right now.

00:25:21.210 --> 00:25:23.760
Maybe one day it'll become
part of Khan Academy.

00:25:23.760 --> 00:25:25.760
But it was catalyzed by
this COVID situation,

00:25:25.760 --> 00:25:27.340
where you have Khan Academy,

00:25:27.340 --> 00:25:30.890
it's a place where kids
and families can go

00:25:30.890 --> 00:25:32.820
to get their practice, to get supports,

00:25:32.820 --> 00:25:34.890
they have hints, they have solutions,

00:25:34.890 --> 00:25:36.560
they have videos, they have articles.

00:25:36.560 --> 00:25:40.260
But what about live sessions
with a real teacher,

00:25:40.260 --> 00:25:41.100
a real tutor?

00:25:41.100 --> 00:25:42.653
And that's what the goal
at Schoolhouse.world is.

00:25:42.653 --> 00:25:44.830
It's a place where students can go

00:25:44.830 --> 00:25:46.710
and pick which areas they need help in,

00:25:46.710 --> 00:25:49.570
and they can get free live
group tutoring sessions

00:25:49.570 --> 00:25:52.960
with vetted tutors who are volunteers.

00:25:52.960 --> 00:25:54.023
So if anyone's interested in that,

00:25:54.023 --> 00:25:56.100
I highly recommend going
to Schoolhouse.world.

00:25:56.100 --> 00:25:57.760
But this is kind of the portfolio of stuff

00:25:57.760 --> 00:25:59.440
that we're trying to do in preparation

00:25:59.440 --> 00:26:02.450
for what's going to
clearly be a very strange

00:26:02.450 --> 00:26:06.570
back-to-school season or year.

00:26:06.570 --> 00:26:08.320
We wanna make sure that
Khan Academy is there

00:26:08.320 --> 00:26:10.880
for as much practice and
learning as possible,

00:26:10.880 --> 00:26:13.580
and we can help provide
some guidance and a view

00:26:13.580 --> 00:26:15.850
of how that can fit into
a more holistic picture

00:26:15.850 --> 00:26:17.653
for teachers and families.

00:26:18.510 --> 00:26:23.187
So let's see, from Facebook, Julie Frisk,

00:26:23.187 --> 00:26:24.850
"Are there best practices
for distance learning

00:26:24.850 --> 00:26:25.890
for high school?

00:26:25.890 --> 00:26:27.780
It seems that they
would be more successful

00:26:27.780 --> 00:26:28.870
if they were taking fewer classes

00:26:28.870 --> 00:26:30.830
rather than the typical nine periods."

00:26:30.830 --> 00:26:33.787
So, Julie, I couldn't agree
more with your intuition.

00:26:33.787 --> 00:26:35.330
I think, frankly, even in normal times,

00:26:35.330 --> 00:26:38.990
nine periods is like that's a lotta breath

00:26:38.990 --> 00:26:40.660
and a lotta stress.

00:26:40.660 --> 00:26:42.430
That's not a good idea.

00:26:42.430 --> 00:26:44.410
It's much better to do fewer courses

00:26:44.410 --> 00:26:45.680
and be able to focus on them,

00:26:45.680 --> 00:26:48.070
and especially in a time of COVID.

00:26:48.070 --> 00:26:50.150
What I've been telling
people at all grade levels,

00:26:50.150 --> 00:26:52.870
at all age levels, is
focus first and foremost

00:26:52.870 --> 00:26:55.720
on the math, the reading, and the writing.

00:26:55.720 --> 00:26:57.480
Make sure those don't atrophy.

00:26:57.480 --> 00:26:59.120
If you can put in even 40,

00:26:59.120 --> 00:27:00.410
if you're a high school student,

00:27:00.410 --> 00:27:01.760
let's call it 45 minutes a day

00:27:01.760 --> 00:27:05.260
of solid active work
practice in each of those,

00:27:05.260 --> 00:27:07.450
math, and reading, and writing every day,

00:27:07.450 --> 00:27:08.920
you're going to not atrophy

00:27:08.920 --> 00:27:10.170
and you'll probably progress,

00:27:10.170 --> 00:27:11.570
and the reading and the writing, for sure,

00:27:11.570 --> 00:27:13.020
can be very low tech.

00:27:13.020 --> 00:27:14.950
It could be go find a book list

00:27:14.950 --> 00:27:16.830
of books appropriate for your grade level

00:27:16.830 --> 00:27:19.030
or whatever reading level you're at,

00:27:19.030 --> 00:27:21.200
read those books, find a group of people

00:27:21.200 --> 00:27:22.300
that you can talk about them with.

00:27:22.300 --> 00:27:24.340
Hopefully, your teacher is
leading you through this,

00:27:24.340 --> 00:27:26.210
but even if you're not
getting that support,

00:27:26.210 --> 00:27:27.700
find some students, some peers,

00:27:27.700 --> 00:27:29.370
who wanna go on this journey with you,

00:27:29.370 --> 00:27:31.930
or some families, if you're
a parent of a high schooler

00:27:31.930 --> 00:27:33.560
that will do this with you.

00:27:33.560 --> 00:27:37.120
The writing, every week, pick
something to write about,

00:27:37.120 --> 00:27:39.150
and you could mix it up through the year.

00:27:39.150 --> 00:27:40.490
I'm gonna write a couple of short stories.

00:27:40.490 --> 00:27:42.360
I'm gonna write a couple
of opinion pieces.

00:27:42.360 --> 00:27:44.180
I'm gonna write an analysis

00:27:45.040 --> 00:27:46.940
of whether we should open schools,

00:27:46.940 --> 00:27:50.580
or something like that,
and put them on a blog,

00:27:50.580 --> 00:27:51.930
put them on Medium or something,

00:27:51.930 --> 00:27:53.640
and send them to all
your friends and family

00:27:53.640 --> 00:27:56.220
and get feedback from
them and ask for feedback.

00:27:56.220 --> 00:27:57.250
How could this be better?

00:27:57.250 --> 00:28:00.060
How can I improve this and iterate on it?

00:28:00.060 --> 00:28:03.080
I think if you do that,
you're not going to atrophy

00:28:03.080 --> 00:28:04.540
and you're actually going
to develop a lot of skills.

00:28:04.540 --> 00:28:07.070
And then once you have your
legs under yourself there,

00:28:07.070 --> 00:28:10.040
then I would layer on maybe one science

00:28:10.040 --> 00:28:14.130
and one social science,

00:28:14.130 --> 00:28:17.260
a history and whatever
science you're working on,

00:28:17.260 --> 00:28:19.200
whether it's physics, chemistry, biology.

00:28:19.200 --> 00:28:21.560
And if you do that, that's really solid,

00:28:21.560 --> 00:28:23.440
especially while we go
through this pandemic,

00:28:23.440 --> 00:28:25.930
and make sure that you
have enough time for sleep,

00:28:25.930 --> 00:28:27.160
enough time for socializing,

00:28:27.160 --> 00:28:29.070
enough time for exercise,
time with your family.

00:28:29.070 --> 00:28:32.000
Because I think the biggest
risk we have right now

00:28:32.000 --> 00:28:33.340
is just people burning out

00:28:33.340 --> 00:28:35.010
and the mental health
issues that might happen

00:28:35.010 --> 00:28:35.843
because of quarantine.

00:28:35.843 --> 00:28:38.480
So, I agree with your intuition

00:28:38.480 --> 00:28:41.350
to kind of focus on doing fewer things,

00:28:41.350 --> 00:28:44.120
but doing them better and
not stressing ourselves out

00:28:44.120 --> 00:28:45.280
or spreading ourselves too thin

00:28:45.280 --> 00:28:47.560
in this very, very difficult time.

00:28:47.560 --> 00:28:50.403
So, it looks like we're almost outta time.

00:28:51.700 --> 00:28:54.263
Let's see if there's any
questions I could answer.

00:28:55.560 --> 00:28:58.082
Let's see, from YouTube,

00:28:58.082 --> 00:29:00.882
there's a lotta numbers in
this username, T0RicksCain3S,

00:29:02.847 --> 00:29:03.820
"Do you have any suggestions

00:29:03.820 --> 00:29:05.470
for remembering factual information

00:29:05.470 --> 00:29:07.440
in subjects like history?"

00:29:07.440 --> 00:29:09.240
I'm not the best memorizer in the world,

00:29:09.240 --> 00:29:11.700
so take anything I have to
say with a grain of salt,

00:29:11.700 --> 00:29:13.660
but what I find is the more ways

00:29:13.660 --> 00:29:15.770
that you can represent
knowledge to yourself,

00:29:15.770 --> 00:29:17.660
and the more connections you can draw,

00:29:17.660 --> 00:29:19.770
the more that you will retain.

00:29:19.770 --> 00:29:20.660
When you look at these people

00:29:20.660 --> 00:29:23.780
who are like "Guinness Book
of World Record" memorizers,

00:29:23.780 --> 00:29:26.300
what they'll tell is you they
have visualizations they do.

00:29:26.300 --> 00:29:28.570
So if they're trying to
memorize a list of objects,

00:29:28.570 --> 00:29:32.797
like a cat, a clock, spaghetti, and shoe,

00:29:35.260 --> 00:29:37.350
the way that they will memorize that

00:29:37.350 --> 00:29:39.550
is that they'll say, "Oh,
okay, I'm going to go,"

00:29:39.550 --> 00:29:42.050
they'll visualize a story
that they walk into a room

00:29:42.050 --> 00:29:45.410
and then the cat comes and it meows.

00:29:45.410 --> 00:29:48.340
And just as it meows, you
notice that there's a clock

00:29:48.340 --> 00:29:52.630
right behind the cat, and
so you enter the room,

00:29:52.630 --> 00:29:54.087
you look at the clock, and you realize,

00:29:54.087 --> 00:29:55.780
"Oh my god, it's time for spaghetti.

00:29:55.780 --> 00:29:57.640
I can smell the spaghetti.

00:29:57.640 --> 00:30:00.947
Let me enter it into the
kitchen and have the spaghetti."

00:30:02.970 --> 00:30:04.800
And the analogy, the
good thing about history

00:30:04.800 --> 00:30:07.205
is that history already
has a narrative with it.

00:30:07.205 --> 00:30:08.560
(chuckling) You don't have to make one up

00:30:08.560 --> 00:30:11.190
with some cat and the clock and spaghetti.

00:30:11.190 --> 00:30:12.840
A history, there are connections.

00:30:12.840 --> 00:30:14.540
And so what I would recommend you do

00:30:14.540 --> 00:30:19.291
is don't just memorize that
World War I started in 1914,

00:30:19.291 --> 00:30:24.291
and Kennedy was inaugurated in 1961,

00:30:26.420 --> 00:30:28.220
just blindly memorize facts.

00:30:28.220 --> 00:30:30.810
Think about what else was
going on in the world.

00:30:30.810 --> 00:30:34.720
Think about, okay, in 1917,

00:30:34.720 --> 00:30:36.180
what was the state of technology?

00:30:36.180 --> 00:30:39.400
Visualize it, think about, wow,

00:30:39.400 --> 00:30:42.783
that was two years before
the Spanish flu hit.

00:30:45.030 --> 00:30:46.530
What was it like to live then?

00:30:47.380 --> 00:30:49.130
What was going on in
other parts of the world

00:30:49.130 --> 00:30:52.210
where maybe they weren't at
war, or other parts of the world

00:30:52.210 --> 00:30:53.390
where your history books aren't covering,

00:30:53.390 --> 00:30:55.460
and the more connections you draw,

00:30:55.460 --> 00:30:57.580
the more that you'll have a frame.

00:30:57.580 --> 00:31:01.160
And then those years and
those dates and those names

00:31:01.160 --> 00:31:06.160
and those places will kind of
very naturally fit into place.

00:31:06.280 --> 00:31:08.910
So, unfortunately, we're all out of time.

00:31:08.910 --> 00:31:10.350
This always goes much faster.

00:31:10.350 --> 00:31:12.873
I guess I like to answer questions.

00:31:13.720 --> 00:31:16.000
But thanks so much for
joining the livestream today.

00:31:16.000 --> 00:31:18.520
Once again, my apologies
for those of you all

00:31:18.520 --> 00:31:19.650
hoping to see Lester Holt.

00:31:19.650 --> 00:31:23.110
That obviously would have been
an improvement over just me,

00:31:23.110 --> 00:31:24.970
but we are hoping to get him soon.

00:31:24.970 --> 00:31:29.920
He had to delay because
of some breaking news.

00:31:29.920 --> 00:31:33.690
But I look forward to
continuing this shortly.

00:31:33.690 --> 00:31:36.824
So, thanks everyone for joining this AMA,

00:31:36.824 --> 00:31:38.524
this "Ask Me Anything" livestream.

